WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:04.520
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:08.500
Let's talk about the mathematical intricacies
of the elections!

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:13.520
Here you can see the shape of the twelfth
congressional district in North Carolina in

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:14.789
the 90's.

00:00:14.789 --> 00:00:18.180
This is not a naturally shaped electoral district,
is it?

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.910
One might say this is more of an abomination.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:26.760
If we try to understand why it has this peculiar
shape, we shall find a remarkable mathematical

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:27.920
mischief.

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:31.460
Have a look at this example of 50 electoral
precincts.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:35.480
The distribution is 60% percent blue, and
40% red.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:40.770
So this means that the blue party should win
the elections and gain seats with the ratio

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:43.110
of 60 to 40, right?

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:45.739
Well, this is not exactly how it works.

00:00:45.739 --> 00:00:50.960
There is a majority decision district by district,
regardless of the vote ratios.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:56.390
If the electoral districts are shaped like
this, then the blue party wins 5 seats to

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:57.390
zero.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:02.640
However, if they are, instead, shaped like
this, the red party wins 3 to 2.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:06.560
Which is kind of mind blowing, because the
votes are the very same.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:09.259
And this is known as the wasted vote effect.

00:01:09.259 --> 00:01:15.119
This term doesn't refer to someone who enters
the voting booth intoxicated, this means that

00:01:15.119 --> 00:01:22.819
one can think of pretty much every vote beyond
50% + 1 to a party in a district, to be irrelevant.

00:01:22.819 --> 00:01:29.459
It doesn't matter if the district is won by
99% of the votes or just by 50% + 1 vote.

00:01:29.459 --> 00:01:32.270
So, the cunning plan is now laid out.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:38.639
What if, instead, we could regroup all these
extra votes to win in a different district

00:01:38.639 --> 00:01:40.219
where we were losing?

00:01:40.219 --> 00:01:44.889
And now, we have ceremoniously arrived to
the definition of Gerrymandering, which is

00:01:44.889 --> 00:01:50.770
the process of manipulating electoral district
boundaries to turn the tide of an election.

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:56.479
The term originates from one of the elections
in the USA in the 1800s, where Governor Elbridge

00:01:56.479 --> 00:02:02.669
Gerry signed a bill to reshape the districts
of Massachusetts in order to favor his party.

00:02:02.669 --> 00:02:08.140
And at that time, understanably, all the papers
and comic artists were up in arms about this

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:09.140
bill.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:11.860
So how does one perform gerrymandering?

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:16.530
Gerrymandering is actually a mathematical
problem of the purest form where we are trying

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:21.430
to maximize the number of seats that we can
win by manipulating the district boundaries

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:22.670
appropriately.

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:28.060
It is important to note that the entire process
relies on a relatively faithful prediction

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:32.690
of the vote distributions per region, which
in many countries, is not really changing

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:34.060
all that much in time.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:38.320
This is a problem that we can solve via standard
optimization techniques.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:44.070
Now, hold on to your papers, and get this:
for instance, we can use Metropolis sampling

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:47.670
to solve this problem, which is, absolutely
stunning.

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:53.270
So far, in an earlier episode, we have used
Metropolis sampling to develop a super efficient

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:59.130
light simulation program to create beautiful
images of virtual scenes, and the very same

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:02.410
technique can also be used to steal an election.

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:07.550
In fact, Metropolis sampling was developed
and used during the Manhattan project, where

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:10.430
the first atomic bomb was created in Los Alamos.

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:15.420
I think it is completely understandable that
the power of mathematics and research still

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:21.560
give many of us sleepless nights, sometimes
delightful, sometimes perilous.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:25.960
It is also important to note that in order
to retain the fairness of the elections in

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:31.530
a district-based system, it is of utmost importance
that these district boundaries are drawn by

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:36.200
an independent organization and that the process
is as transparent as possible.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:39.890
I decided not to cite a concrete paper in
this episode.

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:45.330
If you would like to read up on this topic,
I recommend searching for keywords, like redistricting

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:47.700
and gerrymandering on Google Scholar.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:52.010
Please feel free to post the more interesting
finding of yours in the comments section,

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:54.580
we always have excellent discussions therein.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:58.280
Thanks for watching, and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

